/* Contenido profundo de ob_spanish1 (borrador para revisión del equipo curricular; ver "verify" por unidad). */
window.EL_DEEP = window.EL_DEEP || {};
Object.assign(window.EL_DEEP, {"us:world_language:spanish1:Oct": {"reading": ["Every language begins with a greeting. When you say Buenos días to a neighbor, you are doing more than exchanging sounds: you are telling that person that you see them and that you wish them a good day. In Spanish, greetings change with the time of day. Buenos días is used in the morning, Buenas tardes from midday until evening, and Buenas noches after dark and also when saying goodnight.", "The next step is to introduce yourself. The most common way is Me llamo..., which literally means \"I call myself...\" followed by your name. To ask someone else, you say ¿Cómo te llamas? When you meet a new person, the polite reply is Mucho gusto, \"a pleasure,\" or Encantado if you are a boy and Encantada if you are a girl. To present a friend, use Él es... for a boy and Ella es... for a girl. To ask how someone is, say ¿Cómo estás? and answer Estoy bien, Estoy mal, or Estoy así así, then ask ¿Y tú? To say where you are from, use Soy de... as in Soy de Miami.", "Spanish has two ways of saying \"you\" in a conversation. Tú is informal and is used with friends, classmates, family members and children. Usted is formal and shows respect to elders, teachers, pastors and people you do not know well. With tú you ask ¿Cómo te llamas? and ¿Cómo estás?; with usted you ask ¿Cómo se llama? and ¿Cómo está? The Spanish alphabet is also an important tool, since you will often need to spell your name aloud. Its letters are mostly like English ones, but their names and sounds differ: for example, the vowels are always pronounced the same way (a, e, i, o, u). When you spell, you can say Se escribe... followed by the letters.", "These everyday words connect with a remarkable chapter of church history. In the sixteenth century, most people in Spain could hear the Bible in Latin, but few could read it in the language they spoke at home. Casiodoro de Reina was a Spanish monk from the Seville area who, because of his convictions, left Spain in the 1550s and lived as an exile in several European cities. He translated the Old and New Testaments into Spanish from the Hebrew and Greek, consulting earlier translations. His Bible was printed in Basel, Switzerland, in 1569. It is known as the Biblia del Oso, \"the Bear Bible,\" because the printer's emblem showed a bear (oso) reaching for a honeycomb. It is one of the first complete printed Spanish Bibles and became the foundation of the Reina-Valera Bible.", "What do a greeting and a Bible translation have in common? Both honor a person by speaking to them in their own language. A simple Buenos días says, \"you matter to me.\" Reina's translation said the same thing to millions of Spanish speakers: God's Word was not meant to remain far away in a language few understood. Scripture teaches that every person is made in the image of God (Genesis 1:27). When you learn to greet, introduce and respect others in Spanish, you take the first step of the same kind of service. This year you will also begin the project \"La Brecha y Cómo Alcanzar\" (The Gap and How to Reach It): you will learn who speaks Spanish near you and who still needs to receive good news and a Bible in their own language. We study this history with respect for all, Catholic and evangelical alike."], "glossary": [{"term": "Buenos días", "def": "\"Good morning\"; the greeting used in the morning."}, {"term": "Me llamo", "def": "\"My name is\" (literally \"I call myself\"); used to say your name."}, {"term": "Mucho gusto", "def": "\"Nice to meet you\"; a polite reply when meeting someone."}, {"term": "tú / usted", "def": "Informal and formal ways of saying \"you\"; tú is for friends and family, usted shows respect to elders and strangers."}, {"term": "Soy de...", "def": "\"I am from...\"; used to say your city or country of origin."}, {"term": "Biblia del Oso", "def": "\"Bear Bible\"; the nickname of Casiodoro de Reina's Spanish Bible printed in Basel in 1569, named after the bear on the printer's emblem."}, {"term": "exilio (exile)", "def": "Living outside one's homeland because it is unsafe to stay, as Casiodoro de Reina did for his convictions."}, {"term": "Hasta luego", "def": "\"See you later\"; a friendly way to say goodbye."}], "practice": [{"q": "Write a short dialogue in Spanish in which you greet your pastor in the afternoon and ask how he is. Which form (tú or usted) do you use, and why?", "feedback": "A good answer uses Buenas tardes and the formal question ¿Cómo está usted? because a pastor is an adult who deserves respect. Usted shows courtesy and cultural awareness. With a classmate you would use tú: ¿Cómo estás? Choosing the right form is part of speaking respectfully, not only correctly."}, {"q": "Introduce yourself and a friend in four Spanish sentences, including where each of you is from. Then spell your name using Se escribe...", "feedback": "A model answer is: Me llamo Sara. Soy de Orlando. Él es Pablo. Él es de México. Se escribe S-A-R-A. Check that Él/Ella matches the person, that Soy de and Es de agree with the subject, and that you pronounced the vowels the same way every time."}, {"q": "Why is the Biblia del Oso called by that name, and what did Casiodoro de Reina do before it was printed in 1569?", "feedback": "The printer's emblem showed a bear (oso), so the Bible was nicknamed the Biblia del Oso. Before it was printed, Reina, a Spanish monk who had gone into exile, spent years translating the Old and New Testaments into Spanish from Hebrew and Greek while consulting earlier translations. The name came from the printer; the work came from Reina."}, {"q": "Explain in your own words how greeting someone in their language and translating the Bible into their language express the same kind of respect.", "feedback": "Both actions say that a person matters enough to be addressed in the language of their heart. A greeting opens a conversation; a translation opens God's Word. Both reflect the belief that every person is made in God's image, and both should be done humbly and without looking down on anyone's culture or church tradition."}], "quiz": [{"q": "Which greeting would you use at 8:00 in the morning?", "opts": [{"t": "Buenas noches", "ok": false}, {"t": "Buenos días", "ok": true}, {"t": "Hasta luego", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "You meet a new classmate. Which pair of phrases is the most natural?", "opts": [{"t": "¿Cómo te llamas? / Mucho gusto", "ok": true}, {"t": "Hasta luego / Buenas noches", "ok": false}, {"t": "¿Cómo está usted? / Me llamo tú", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "Why does the printed Spanish Bible of 1569 carry the nickname \"del Oso\"?", "opts": [{"t": "Because Reina was born in a village called Oso", "ok": false}, {"t": "Because it was printed in a city named Oso", "ok": false}, {"t": "Because the printer's emblem was a bear", "ok": true}]}, {"q": "Which sentence correctly uses the formal usted to ask how a teacher is?", "opts": [{"t": "¿Cómo estás?", "ok": false}, {"t": "¿Cómo está usted?", "ok": true}, {"t": "¿Cómo te llamas?", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "What is the best explanation of the connection between a greeting and Reina's translation?", "opts": [{"t": "Both honor people by speaking to them in their own language", "ok": true}, {"t": "Both are only grammar exercises", "ok": false}, {"t": "Both were created in the twentieth century", "ok": false}]}], "verify": ["Reina's birthplace (traditionally Montemolín, Extremadura) and the exact year he left Spain (c. 1557).", "Whether the Biblia del Oso (1569) may be called 'the first complete printed Spanish Bible': the text uses 'one of the first' because the Ferrara Bible (1553) and medieval versions existed.", "Printer's emblem of a bear reaching for a honeycomb (Basel, 1569): confirm the printer and emblem description.", "General claim that the Reina-Valera is widely used in Spanish-speaking evangelical churches: no percentages given."], "reading_es": "Hola. Me llamo Ana. Soy de Miami. Mucho gusto. Por la mañana digo: Buenos días. Por la tarde digo: Buenas tardes. Por la noche digo: Buenas noches. Casiodoro de Reina fue un monje español. Él tradujo la Biblia al español. La Biblia se llama «la Biblia del Oso». Es una Biblia para las familias que hablan español. Dios nos ama a todos. Yo saludo con respeto a las personas. Mi amigo se llama Luis. Él es de México. ¿Cómo estás? Estoy bien."}, "us:world_language:spanish1:Nov": {"reading": ["Family is one of the first topics every Spanish student learns, because in Hispanic cultures the family is the center of social life. The basic words are la madre, el padre, el hermano and la hermana, el abuelo and la abuela, el tío and la tía, el primo and la prima, and el hijo and la hija. Los hermanos can mean \"brothers\" or \"brothers and sisters.\" To talk about family you also need possessive adjectives: mi (my), tu (your) and su (his, her, your formal, their), as in mi madre, tu abuelo, su prima. In the plural they become mis, tus and sus: mis tíos, tus primos.", "To describe people, Spanish uses the verb ser (to be): yo soy, tú eres, él o ella es, nosotros somos, ellos son. Adjectives must agree with the noun in gender and number. A boy can be alto, simpático and trabajador; a girl is alta, simpática and trabajadora; two girls are altas, simpáticas and trabajadoras. Compare Mi hermano es alto with Mi hermana es alta and Mis primos son simpáticos. To give an age, Spanish does not use \"to be\" but tener (to have): Tengo doce años literally means \"I have twelve years.\" You will practice numbers from zero to one hundred so that you can say ages, addresses and phone numbers. People in Spanish-speaking countries also traditionally use two last names, from the father and the mother, honoring both sides of the family.", "Large family gatherings, with grandparents, aunts, uncles and cousins around one table, are a cherished tradition throughout the Spanish-speaking world. This closeness helps us understand a painful chapter of the sixteenth century. In the city of Seville a group of monks and laypeople, linked to the monastery of San Isidoro del Campo, studied the Bible with deep care and came to hold reformed convictions. At that time the Spanish Inquisition, an institution of the Spanish crown and the Church of that period, investigated people suspected of holding beliefs it considered contrary to the faith. Several members of the Seville group were tried, and around 1559 to 1562 some of them were punished in public ceremonies known as autos de fe.", "Others escaped. Casiodoro de Reina, a monk of San Isidoro, and Cipriano de Valera, who is also linked to the Seville circle, fled abroad around the late 1550s. They became exiles, which meant leaving their homes, their friends and their relatives behind, perhaps never to see them again. Reina carried his work to cities in northern Europe, and Valera later spent many years in England and the Netherlands. In exile they kept working on what mattered most to them: giving Spanish speakers the Bible in their own language. Valera later revised Reina's translation, and his revision was published in Amsterdam in 1602. Today we call the combined work the Reina-Valera Bible.", "How should a Christian remember such events? Honestly, because the truth about history matters, and humbly, because no group of people has been free from mistakes. Our Catholic neighbors and friends, and all Spaniards, are people made in the image of God (Genesis 1:27), and we do not blame today's families for the decisions of institutions in the past. The Bible reminds us to \"be quick to listen, slow to speak\" (James 1:19). Learning the vocabulary of family also teaches us what exile cost: an empty chair at the table. And it gives us a model for the project \"La Brecha y Cómo Alcanzar\": every person you want to serve has a family, a story and a name worth learning."], "glossary": [{"term": "la familia", "def": "\"The family\"; in Hispanic cultures often includes grandparents, aunts, uncles and cousins."}, {"term": "mi, tu, su", "def": "Possessive adjectives meaning \"my,\" \"your\" and \"his/her/their\"; they add -s in the plural (mis, tus, sus)."}, {"term": "ser", "def": "A verb meaning \"to be\" used to describe who people are and what they are like: Soy alto, Ella es simpática."}, {"term": "Tengo ... años", "def": "\"I am ... years old\" (literally \"I have ... years\"); uses tener, not ser."}, {"term": "San Isidoro del Campo", "def": "A monastery near Seville where some monks studied the Bible and held reformed convictions in the sixteenth century."}, {"term": "Inquisición (Inquisition)", "def": "A tribunal of the Spanish crown and the Church of that time that investigated beliefs it considered contrary to the faith."}, {"term": "auto de fe", "def": "A public ceremony in which the Inquisition announced the sentences of those it had tried."}, {"term": "exilio (exile)", "def": "Forced or necessary absence from one's homeland and family."}], "practice": [{"q": "Describe two members of your family in Spanish using ser and adjectives that agree (for example, a grandmother and a cousin). Give an age with tener.", "feedback": "A good answer might be: Mi abuela es simpática y trabajadora. Mi primo es alto. Tiene catorce años. The adjectives must match gender and number (simpática for a woman, alto for a man), and the age uses Tiene/Tengo, not Es/Soy."}, {"q": "Translate into Spanish: \"My cousins are tall and friendly. I am twelve years old.\" Explain the two agreement choices you made.", "feedback": "The sentences are Mis primos son altos y simpáticos. Tengo doce años. The possessive and the adjectives are plural (mis, altos, simpáticos) to agree with primos, and the age uses tener. If the cousins were all girls, the adjectives would be altas and simpáticas."}, {"q": "Who were the Seville reformers, and why did some of them go into exile? Answer in four to five sentences, using respectful language toward everyone involved.", "feedback": "They were monks and laypeople connected to San Isidoro del Campo who studied the Bible and held reformed convictions. The Spanish Inquisition investigated many of them and some were punished in autos de fe around 1559-1562, so others, like Reina and Valera, fled abroad. A respectful answer describes events without insulting Catholics or blaming present-day families."}, {"q": "What does the family vocabulary of this unit help us understand about the cost of exile for Reina and Valera?", "feedback": "Exile meant leaving parents, siblings, relatives and friends behind, the very people we describe with words like madre, hermano and tío. Understanding family as the center of life in Hispanic culture helps us feel what they gave up, and why their persistence in translating the Bible is remarkable."}], "quiz": [{"q": "How do you say \"my sisters are tall\" in Spanish?", "opts": [{"t": "Mi hermanas es alto", "ok": false}, {"t": "Mis hermanas son alto", "ok": false}, {"t": "Mis hermanas son altas", "ok": true}]}, {"q": "Which sentence correctly gives an age?", "opts": [{"t": "Soy trece años", "ok": false}, {"t": "Tengo trece años", "ok": true}, {"t": "Estoy trece años", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "Why is a person often called by two last names in the Spanish-speaking world?", "opts": [{"t": "To honor both the father's and the mother's family", "ok": true}, {"t": "Because they have two churches", "ok": false}, {"t": "Because the law requires a nickname", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "What was San Isidoro del Campo?", "opts": [{"t": "A university in Basel", "ok": false}, {"t": "A printing house in Amsterdam", "ok": false}, {"t": "A monastery near Seville where some monks studied the Bible", "ok": true}]}, {"q": "What is the most accurate description of the attitude a Christian should take when studying the Inquisition?", "opts": [{"t": "Angry at all Spaniards", "ok": false}, {"t": "Honest about the past and respectful toward Catholic neighbors today", "ok": true}, {"t": "Silent, as if nothing happened", "ok": false}]}], "verify": ["Dates of the Seville autos de fe (c. 1559-1562) and of the flight of the San Isidoro group (c. 1557).", "Number of people punished: no figures are stated; confirm with a historian before adding any.", "Birthplace of Cipriano de Valera (traditionally Fregenal de la Sierra) and whether he belonged to the San Isidoro monastery or the wider Seville circle.", "Valera's residence in England and the Netherlands and the 1602 publication in Amsterdam of his revision."], "reading_es": "Esta es mi familia. Mi madre se llama Rosa. Mi padre se llama Pedro. Tengo un hermano y una abuela. Mi hermano es alto. Mi abuela es simpática. Tengo diez años. Hace mucho tiempo, unos cristianos de Sevilla leían la Biblia. Algunos tuvieron que salir de España. Reina y Valera dejaron a su familia. Ellos trabajaron para tener una Biblia en español. Yo quiero respetar a todas las personas. Mi familia es muy importante."}, "us:world_language:spanish1:Dic": {"reading": ["Numbers, dates and time are the tools we use to organize life, and Spanish handles them with a regular pattern. After the numbers from zero to one hundred, you learn the hundreds: cien, doscientos, trescientos, cuatrocientos, quinientos, seiscientos, setecientos, ochocientos, novecientos and mil. Note that the hundreds agree in gender, so you say doscientas personas but doscientos libros. The days of the week (lunes, martes, miércoles, jueves, viernes, sábado, domingo) and the months (enero to diciembre) are written in lowercase. To give today's date, you say Hoy es el diez de diciembre, using the pattern day plus de plus month. To ask the time you say ¿Qué hora es? and answer Es la una for one o'clock but Son las tres for any other hour; half past is y media and a quarter past is y cuarto, as in Son las tres y media.", "December is also a good month to study Christmas vocabulary. In Spanish-speaking countries Christmas, la Navidad, is celebrated in many ways. On Nochebuena, December 24, many families gather for a late dinner and often attend a Misa del Gallo or a candlelight service. Many homes display a nacimiento, a nativity scene with figures of Mary, Joseph and the baby Jesus, which in some countries is more important than a tree. In Mexico there are posadas, nine evenings of processions that remember the journey to Bethlehem; in Spain and in many other countries children wait for January 6, el Día de los Reyes Magos, when the Three Kings are remembered. Comparing celebrations shows both diversity and shared faith.", "The Christmas story is told in Luke 2, and it is read in Spanish in Catholic and evangelical churches alike. The words of the Bible come to us in Spanish largely through the labor of two men. We have already met Casiodoro de Reina, whose Biblia del Oso was printed in Basel in 1569. In the decades that followed, a second exile from the Seville circle, Cipriano de Valera, devoted himself to improving that work. Valera spent many years in northern Europe, and according to tradition he worked for roughly twenty years on a careful revision of Reina's text, comparing it with the original languages and other translations. His revision was published in Amsterdam in 1602, and its name, Reina-Valera, joins the names of both men.", "The story of the Reina-Valera did not end in 1602. Because languages change, later generations carefully revised the text to keep it clear, and revisions have appeared over the centuries, for example in 1909 and 1960, among others. What remains constant is the principle that moved Reina and Valera: that people should be able to read God's Word in the language they understand. Translating is humble work that requires patience and a willingness to correct oneself, like learning verb endings and numbers.", "Why does this matter at Christmas? The Gospel of John says that the Word became flesh and dwelt among us (John 1:14). God did not remain distant; he came near in a way that people could see and understand. A translator follows the same logic in a small way: he brings the Scriptures near, in words that ordinary families can read in their own kitchen. As you practice dates and times, you can also make a calendar for the Gap project, writing in Spanish when a church or neighbor might be invited to a Christmas meeting, and remembering that hospitality is a form of testimony."], "glossary": [{"term": "Hoy es el...", "def": "\"Today is the...\"; the pattern for giving the date, as in Hoy es el diez de diciembre."}, {"term": "¿Qué hora es?", "def": "\"What time is it?\" Answered with Es la una or Son las dos, tres, etc."}, {"term": "la Navidad", "def": "Christmas; in Spanish-speaking countries it often includes Nochebuena (December 24) and the nacimiento."}, {"term": "el nacimiento", "def": "A nativity scene displayed at home or church at Christmas; also means \"birth.\""}, {"term": "los Reyes Magos", "def": "The Three Kings (Magi) remembered on January 6 in many Spanish-speaking countries."}, {"term": "revisión (revision)", "def": "A careful correction of an existing translation; Cipriano de Valera revised Reina's Bible, published in 1602."}, {"term": "Reina-Valera", "def": "The name of the Spanish Bible that joins Casiodoro de Reina's translation (1569) and Cipriano de Valera's revision (1602), revised again in later centuries."}, {"term": "ciento / cien", "def": "\"One hundred\"; cien stands alone or before nouns, ciento is used within larger numbers."}], "practice": [{"q": "Write today's date and the time as you would say them in Spanish (for example, Hoy es el quince de diciembre. Son las cuatro y cuarto). Explain why you use Son and not Es for four o'clock.", "feedback": "Time uses the plural Son for every hour except one, because the phrase refers to \"the hours\" (las cuatro), while one o'clock is Es la una. Dates use day + de + month, with lowercase month names. Students should check that numbers are spelled correctly."}, {"q": "Compare one Christmas tradition from Spain or Mexico with one from your own family. Write three sentences using Navidad, Nochebuena or nacimiento.", "feedback": "A good answer names a tradition (posadas, the nacimiento, Nochebuena dinner) and describes it respectfully, then compares it with the student's own custom. The goal is to notice both differences and a shared center: remembering Christ's birth. Avoid saying one tradition is better."}, {"q": "What is the relationship between Reina's Biblia del Oso (1569) and Valera's Bible (1602)?", "feedback": "Reina made the original translation printed in Basel in 1569; Valera, an exile who had also left Spain, revised it over many years and published it in Amsterdam in 1602. That is why the combined text is called Reina-Valera. Later revisions kept updating the language while preserving that foundation."}, {"q": "Why did Reina and Valera spend decades on a single book, and what does John 1:14 add to your answer?", "feedback": "They believed that Scripture should be understood by ordinary people in their own language, so they did patient work that took decades. John 1:14 says that the Word became flesh and lived among us, showing a God who comes near. Their translation was an attempt to bring the Word near to Spanish speakers."}], "quiz": [{"q": "Which sentence correctly tells the time 3:30?", "opts": [{"t": "Son las tres y media", "ok": true}, {"t": "Es la tres y media", "ok": false}, {"t": "Son la tres media", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "What is the Spanish name for the nativity scene set up at Christmas?", "opts": [{"t": "la cena", "ok": false}, {"t": "el abrigo", "ok": false}, {"t": "el nacimiento", "ok": true}]}, {"q": "What did Cipriano de Valera do with Reina's Bible?", "opts": [{"t": "He destroyed it", "ok": false}, {"t": "He carefully revised it, and the revision was published in 1602", "ok": true}, {"t": "He printed it for the first time in 1569", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "Why were later revisions of the Reina-Valera made, for example in 1909 and 1960?", "opts": [{"t": "To keep the language clear as Spanish changed", "ok": true}, {"t": "To remove the Old Testament", "ok": false}, {"t": "To shorten the Bible", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "What principle moved both Reina and Valera?", "opts": [{"t": "Only scholars should read the Bible", "ok": false}, {"t": "Spanish should be replaced by Latin", "ok": false}, {"t": "People should read God's Word in the language they understand", "ok": true}]}], "verify": ["Valera's roughly twenty years of work and the exact publication years (New Testament 1596?, full Bible 1602).", "Revision years of the Reina-Valera (1862, 1909, 1960, 1995) and which one is most used today.", "Birthplaces of Reina and Valera.", "Christmas customs by country (posadas in Mexico, Día de Reyes in Spain and elsewhere): confirm generalizations before using."], "reading_es": "Hoy es el diez de diciembre. Son las tres y media. En diciembre es la Navidad. La familia está en casa. Hay un nacimiento con María, José y el niño Jesús. Cipriano de Valera trabajó muchos años en la Biblia. En 1602 salió su Biblia. Es la Biblia de Reina y Valera. Gracias, Dios, por la Biblia en español. Mi familia lee la Biblia en Navidad. Es la fiesta de Jesús."}, "us:world_language:spanish1:Feb": {"reading": ["School vocabulary is among the most useful in a new language because you use it every day. In Spanish you study las matemáticas, la ciencia, la historia, el inglés, el español and la música. In your backpack, la mochila, you carry el libro, el cuaderno, el lápiz, el bolígrafo and la regla. Most Spanish nouns follow the -o and -a pattern, but some surprise English speakers: el mapa and el día are masculine even though they end in -a. Learn each article together with its noun.", "Spanish verbs are organized in families. The regular -ar verbs, such as estudiar, hablar, trabajar and necesitar, share the same endings in the present tense: -o, -as, -a, -amos, -an. For example, yo estudio, tú estudias, él estudia, nosotros estudiamos and ellos estudian. Because the ending tells you who is acting, Spanish often omits the subject pronoun and says Estudio matemáticas. To say what you like, Spanish uses gustar in a structure that surprises English speakers: Me gusta la historia means \"history pleases me,\" and the verb changes with what is liked: Me gustan las ciencias. To say you do not like something, put no before the verb: No me gustan las ciencias.", "Schools in Spanish-speaking countries and in the United States are similar in many ways, but they differ in schedules, uniforms, and customs. In many Hispanic countries students wear uniforms and may have a long midday break, while in the United States most students eat lunch at school and finish earlier. Comparing them is a way of seeing that no single culture holds all the answers, a skill the ACTFL standards call Comparisons. It is also a way of appreciating how education changes the lives of families, a theme that connects directly with the history of the church in Spanish America.", "In the early nineteenth century, a Scottish educator named James Thomson, known in Spanish as Diego Thomson, traveled through Spanish America as an agent of the British and Foreign Bible Society, which had been founded in 1804. He arrived at a historic moment, when many countries were seeking independence from Spain and building new schools and governments. Thomson visited several countries, including Argentina, Chile, Peru, Colombia and Mexico, and spoke with local leaders, teachers and officials. He promoted the Lancasterian system of mutual instruction, in which older or more advanced students helped teach younger ones to read. Because the system required few teachers and little money, it could spread quickly in places where trained teachers were scarce.", "Thomson's goal was not only literacy but also access to Scripture. He believed that when people learned to read, they should have good books to read, and he encouraged the distribution of the Bible and portions of it, including, in general terms, in languages of Indigenous peoples. We should be careful not to exaggerate what he accomplished, since historians continue to study the details. But the pattern is clear: teaching children to read and giving them Scripture in their own language went together. This idea echoes Psalm 119:105: \"Your word is a lamp to my feet and a light to my path.\" For you, school vocabulary becomes a way to serve. In the Gap project, you can write a short list in Spanish of what a child needs to learn to read, la escuela, el libro, la Biblia, and pray for those who teach."], "glossary": [{"term": "la mochila", "def": "\"The backpack\"; used to carry school supplies."}, {"term": "estudiar", "def": "\"To study\"; a regular -ar verb: estudio, estudias, estudia, estudiamos, estudian."}, {"term": "gustar", "def": "\"To be pleasing\"; used for likes and dislikes: Me gusta la historia, Me gustan las ciencias."}, {"term": "Diego (James) Thomson", "def": "A Scottish educator and agent of the British and Foreign Bible Society who traveled in Spanish America in the early nineteenth century."}, {"term": "escuela lancasteriana (Lancasterian school)", "def": "A school using mutual instruction, in which older students helped teach younger ones; promoted by Thomson."}, {"term": "Sociedad Bíblica (Bible society)", "def": "An organization founded to print and distribute Scripture at low cost, such as the British and Foreign Bible Society (1804)."}, {"term": "independencia (independence)", "def": "The political movements of the early nineteenth century in which Spanish-American countries separated from Spain."}], "practice": [{"q": "Write four sentences in Spanish about your school day using estudiar, hablar or necesitar. Include one sentence with gustar.", "feedback": "A model answer: Estudio matemáticas. Mi amigo habla inglés. Necesito un cuaderno. Me gusta la historia. Check that the verb endings match the subject and that gustar agrees with the thing liked (gusta for singular, gustan for plural)."}, {"q": "Compare school in a Spanish-speaking country and in the United States in three sentences, without saying that one is better.", "feedback": "A good comparison names concrete differences (uniforms, schedules, lunch) and states them neutrally. The point of the exercise is cultural understanding: both systems aim to teach children, and differences are not defects."}, {"q": "Who was Diego (James) Thomson, and what two things did he promote in Spanish America?", "feedback": "He was a Scottish educator working for the British and Foreign Bible Society. He promoted Lancasterian mutual-instruction schools, where older students helped teach younger ones, and he encouraged the distribution of Scripture, including in general terms in Indigenous languages."}, {"q": "Why did teaching reading and distributing Scripture go together in Thomson's work? Connect your answer with Psalm 119:105.", "feedback": "A Bible cannot be read by someone who cannot read, and a reader needs worthwhile books. Thomson linked schools and Scripture so that learning would serve both knowledge and faith. Psalm 119:105 describes God's Word as a lamp that guides a person's steps, which is exactly what a reader needs."}], "quiz": [{"q": "Which sentence means \"I like the sciences\"?", "opts": [{"t": "Me gusta las ciencias", "ok": false}, {"t": "Me gustan las ciencias", "ok": true}, {"t": "Yo gusto las ciencias", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "What is the correct form of estudiar with \"we\"?", "opts": [{"t": "estudiamos", "ok": true}, {"t": "estudias", "ok": false}, {"t": "estudian", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "What was the Lancasterian system?", "opts": [{"t": "A system of private tutors for the rich", "ok": false}, {"t": "A method of teaching Latin only", "ok": false}, {"t": "Mutual instruction in which older students helped teach younger ones", "ok": true}]}, {"q": "Who sent Diego Thomson to Spanish America?", "opts": [{"t": "The Spanish Inquisition", "ok": false}, {"t": "The British and Foreign Bible Society (as its agent)", "ok": true}, {"t": "The American Bible Society", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "Which statement about Thomson is stated with proper caution in this unit?", "opts": [{"t": "He encouraged Scripture distribution in general terms, including in Indigenous languages", "ok": true}, {"t": "He translated every Indigenous language himself", "ok": false}, {"t": "He founded the British and Foreign Bible Society", "ok": false}]}], "verify": ["Exact years and countries of Thomson's visits (Argentina c. 1818, Chile 1821, Peru 1822, Colombia, Mexico, others).", "Whether Scripture was used as a reading text in all his schools.", "Specific Indigenous-language projects he supported (for example Quechua): state only in general terms.", "Founding year of the British and Foreign Bible Society (1804)."], "reading_es": "Yo estudio en la escuela. Me gustan las matemáticas y la historia. En mi mochila hay un libro, un cuaderno y un lápiz. Mi maestra habla español. Diego Thomson fue un maestro de Escocia. Él ayudó a abrir escuelas en América. Los niños aprendieron a leer. También leyeron la Biblia. Es bueno leer. Yo quiero leer la Biblia en mi casa. Doy gracias por mi escuela."}, "us:world_language:spanish1:Mar": {"reading": ["Meals are one of the best ways to learn a culture, because eating together expresses friendship. In Spanish, the three main meals are el desayuno (breakfast), el almuerzo (lunch) and la cena (dinner). Typical foods include el arroz, los frijoles, el pan, la fruta, la carne, el pollo, la ensalada and el agua. Mealtimes vary across the Spanish-speaking world: in Spain lunch is often later than in the United States and dinner can be after nine at night, while in Mexico, Colombia and the Caribbean the main meal of the day is frequently at midday. Typical dishes include tortillas and tacos in Mexico, tortilla española and paella in Spain, arepas in Colombia and Venezuela, and rice and beans in several Caribbean countries; the details change from region to region.", "The present tense of -er and -ir verbs follows patterns similar to those of -ar verbs. For -er verbs such as comer and beber the endings are -o, -es, -e, -emos, -en: yo como, tú comes, él come, nosotros comemos, ellos comen. For -ir verbs such as vivir and abrir the endings are -o, -es, -e, -imos, -en. Notice that only the nosotros form differs between the two families. In a restaurant, polite requests use the conditional: Quisiera una sopa, Me gustaría agua, or La cuenta, por favor. Saying grace before eating, dar gracias, is a custom that unites many Hispanic families; a simple prayer, Gracias, Señor, por esta comida, teaches vocabulary while expressing gratitude.", "The theme of sharing food and hospitality leads naturally to the story of the Bible societies and their colporteurs. In the early nineteenth century many Christians believed that Scripture should be available to ordinary people at a price they could afford. The British and Foreign Bible Society was founded in 1804, and the American Bible Society followed in 1816. Their purpose was to print and distribute Bibles in many languages, and they relied on volunteers, donors and translators on several continents. Today, the United Bible Societies, a fellowship formed in 1946, continues translation and distribution.", "A colporteur was a traveling distributor of Bibles and Christian books. The word comes from French and refers to someone who carries goods by hand or on the back. In the nineteenth century colporteurs visited towns, villages and rural areas, including in Latin America and Spain, carrying portions of Scripture in the local language. Their work was demanding. They walked or rode for long distances, depended on the hospitality of strangers for a meal and a bed, and sometimes faced suspicion or opposition. Historians describe that many of them sold Scriptures at low prices rather than simply giving them away, though practices varied, and details differ by country and period.", "What do meals have to do with colporteurs? Hospitality along the road was part of their mission. A bowl of beans offered by a family and a conversation at the table often opened the door for a copy of the Gospels to be read aloud. Jesus himself shared meals with ordinary people and said, \"I was a stranger and you welcomed me\" (Matthew 25:35). When you learn to order food, thank someone for a meal, and say a simple grace in Spanish, you practice the same kind of welcome. Remember that people of different traditions, including Catholic neighbors, have also offered generous hospitality to travelers, and that our task is to be grateful, honest and respectful as we study how Scripture reached homes in Spanish."], "glossary": [{"term": "el desayuno / el almuerzo / la cena", "def": "Breakfast, lunch and dinner."}, {"term": "comer, beber, vivir", "def": "Regular -er and -ir verbs: como, bebes, vive."}, {"term": "Quisiera... / Me gustaría...", "def": "Polite ways of ordering or asking for something (\"I would like...\")."}, {"term": "La cuenta, por favor", "def": "\"The check, please\"; used at the end of a meal in a restaurant."}, {"term": "colportor (colporteur)", "def": "A traveling distributor of Bibles and Christian books who visited towns and rural areas in the nineteenth century."}, {"term": "Sociedad Bíblica (Bible society)", "def": "An organization that prints and distributes Scripture and supports translation; examples are the BFBS (1804) and the ABS (1816)."}, {"term": "hospitalidad (hospitality)", "def": "Welcoming strangers with food and shelter, a practice that often supported colporteurs on the road."}], "practice": [{"q": "Write a short restaurant dialogue in Spanish in which you order a meal and ask for the check. Use Quisiera or Me gustaría.", "feedback": "A model answer: Camarero: ¿Qué desean? Cliente: Quisiera arroz con pollo y agua, por favor. ... Cliente: La cuenta, por favor. The answer should use polite forms and the correct verb endings. Check that por favor and gracias appear."}, {"q": "Conjugate comer and vivir with yo, tú and nosotros, and explain the only difference between the -er and -ir families in the present tense.", "feedback": "Comer: como, comes, comemos. Vivir: vivo, vives, vivimos. The only difference is the nosotros form: -emos for -er verbs and -imos for -ir verbs. Other endings are the same."}, {"q": "What were the BFBS, the ABS and the United Bible Societies, and what shared goal did they have?", "feedback": "The British and Foreign Bible Society (1804) and the American Bible Society (1816) were founded to print and distribute Bibles at affordable prices, and the United Bible Societies (1946) is a fellowship that continues translation and distribution. Their shared goal was to put Scripture into the hands of people in their own languages."}, {"q": "Why was hospitality important to the work of a colporteur, and how can you practice a similar welcome today?", "feedback": "Colporteurs traveled long distances and often depended on families for food and lodging, so hospitality made their journeys possible and created conversation. Today you can welcome new neighbors, say a simple grace in Spanish, and greet people in their language with the humility of a guest."}], "quiz": [{"q": "How do you say \"we eat rice\"?", "opts": [{"t": "Comimos arroz", "ok": false}, {"t": "Comen arroz", "ok": false}, {"t": "Comemos arroz", "ok": true}]}, {"q": "Which phrase is a polite way to order a drink?", "opts": [{"t": "Dame agua ya", "ok": false}, {"t": "Quisiera agua, por favor", "ok": true}, {"t": "Abro agua, por favor", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "What was a colporteur?", "opts": [{"t": "A traveling distributor of Bibles and Christian books", "ok": true}, {"t": "A pastor who never traveled", "ok": false}, {"t": "A Bible printer in Basel", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "Which year is associated with the founding of the British and Foreign Bible Society?", "opts": [{"t": "1569", "ok": false}, {"t": "1946", "ok": false}, {"t": "1804", "ok": true}]}, {"q": "How does the unit connect meals with the colporteurs' work?", "opts": [{"t": "Colporteurs sold only food", "ok": false}, {"t": "Hospitality along the road supported their journeys", "ok": true}, {"t": "Meals were forbidden to them", "ok": false}]}], "verify": ["Founding years: BFBS 1804, ABS 1816, UBS 1946; number of countries served by UBS (stated as 'more than a hundred': confirm).", "Whether colporteurs mostly sold rather than gave Scriptures: stated as 'many sold at low prices' with variation; confirm.", "Regional dishes and mealtimes listed (tortilla española, arepas, tacos, rice and beans) are generalizations: confirm with local sources.", "Any specific Spanish or Latin American colporteur names: none are given."], "reading_es": "Por la mañana tomo el desayuno. Como pan y fruta. Al mediodía tomo el almuerzo. Como arroz y frijoles. Bebo agua. Doy gracias a Dios por la comida. Hace mucho tiempo, unos hombres viajaron con Biblias. Se llamaban colportores. Caminaron por pueblos y campos. Las familias les dieron comida y casa. Yo también quiero ser amable. Bienvenidos a mi mesa."}, "us:world_language:spanish1:Abr": {"reading": ["Learning to move around a city in Spanish is a practical skill. The places in a town include la iglesia, la escuela, el banco, el parque, la tienda, el hospital, la biblioteca, el supermercado and la plaza. Two verbs are essential. The verb estar tells where something is: El banco está cerca de la iglesia; Mis padres están en casa. The verb ir tells where someone is going and is irregular: voy, vas, va, vamos, van. The phrase ir a plus a place gives Voy a la tienda and Vamos a la iglesia; when a masculine noun follows, a + el contracts to al: Voy al parque. To ask where something is, say ¿Dónde está el banco? and to understand the answer you need prepositions and directions: a la derecha, a la izquierda, todo recto, cerca de, lejos de, al lado de, enfrente de.", "Reading simple signs and maps is part of cultural literacy. Spanish place names fill the United States: Los Ángeles, San Antonio, Santa Fe, Florida, Colorado, and many others, because Spanish explorers, settlers and missionaries named places long before the country existed. Today bilingual signs such as Salida (Exit), Abierto (Open) and Bienvenidos (Welcome) help neighbors who speak Spanish at home. When you read a sign in Spanish, you notice that Spanish speakers are part of your own community and that welcome can be written on a wall as well as spoken.", "The theme of the city also leads us to the church in Spain today. In Spain, many evangelical congregations and entities are united through the Federación de Entidades Religiosas Evangélicas de España, abbreviated FEREDE, which brings together churches and organizations of various backgrounds. Evangelical Christians are a small minority in Spain, a country with a long Catholic heritage. The Spanish constitution of 1978 recognized religious freedom, and a Cooperation Agreement between the State and the evangelical entities was approved in 1992, which gave legal standing to evangelical churches in matters such as places of worship and religious teaching; the dates should be confirmed. Today evangelicals and Catholics live as neighbors and friends.", "According to figures cited by Chanak and attributed to the FEREDE, which should be verified, there are about 4,400 evangelical places of worship, lugares de culto, in Spain. The same source reports that about 3,000 municipalities, which is a large share of the country's towns, have no evangelical witness, municipios sin testimonio evangélico. This situation is often described as the España vaciada, \"emptied Spain,\" because many rural communities have lost population as young people move to cities. If these figures are confirmed, they help us see two realities: a network of congregations exists, and yet there are many small towns where no evangelical church is present. They are por verificar, so we present them as estimates.", "How should a student respond to such a gap? First, with humility and respect: a town without an evangelical church is not a town without God's love, since many Catholic neighbors there live their faith faithfully. Second, with curiosity: who lives there, what are their needs, and how might hospitality and service be offered? Jesus said, \"The harvest is plentiful, but the laborers are few\" (Matthew 9:37). In the project \"La Brecha y Cómo Alcanzar,\" this month you begin to locate the gap on a map. You might draw a simple map of your city with the church, school, library and a place where a Spanish-speaking family may need help. You practice the directions you have learned and prepare to describe the place to someone else in Spanish: la iglesia está a la derecha del banco."], "glossary": [{"term": "¿Dónde está...?", "def": "\"Where is...?\"; uses estar for location."}, {"term": "ir a", "def": "\"To go to\"; irregular verb: voy, vas, va, vamos, van. A + el contracts to al."}, {"term": "a la derecha / a la izquierda / todo recto", "def": "To the right, to the left, straight ahead; used to give directions."}, {"term": "FEREDE", "def": "Federación de Entidades Religiosas Evangélicas de España, a federation that brings together evangelical churches and entities in Spain."}, {"term": "lugares de culto", "def": "Places of worship; according to FEREDE figures cited by Chanak (por verificar), about 4,400 evangelical places of worship in Spain."}, {"term": "municipios sin testimonio evangélico", "def": "Municipalities without an evangelical witness; according to figures cited by Chanak (por verificar), about 3,000 in Spain."}, {"term": "España vaciada", "def": "\"Emptied Spain\"; a phrase describing rural areas that have lost population."}], "practice": [{"q": "Write directions in Spanish from your church to the nearest library using at least three expressions from this unit.", "feedback": "A model answer: Sal de la iglesia. Ve todo recto. Gira a la izquierda. La biblioteca está al lado del parque. Directions should use commands or present-tense forms and expressions such as a la derecha, cerca de, enfrente de. Clear order matters more than length."}, {"q": "Explain the difference between estar and ir with one example sentence each.", "feedback": "Estar describes location or condition: La escuela está cerca. Ir describes movement toward a place: Voy a la escuela. When the place is masculine, use al: Voy al parque. Students should note that ir is irregular while estar has its own pattern."}, {"q": "What do the FEREDE figures cited by Chanak say about evangelical presence in Spain, and why should they be treated with caution?", "feedback": "They say that there are about 4,400 evangelical places of worship and that about 3,000 municipalities have no evangelical witness. These numbers come secondhand through Chanak and are marked por verificar, so they must be treated as estimates until confirmed. Good scholarship names the source and the uncertainty."}, {"q": "How can a Christian student speak respectfully about the evangelical minority in Spain and Catholic neighbors at the same time?", "feedback": "A respectful answer recognizes that Spain has a long Catholic heritage, that evangelicals are a small minority, and that Catholic neighbors are friends and not opponents. It focuses on service, hospitality and honesty instead of competition, and avoids suggesting that a town without an evangelical church lacks faith or goodness."}], "quiz": [{"q": "Which sentence correctly says \"I am going to the park\"?", "opts": [{"t": "Voy al parque", "ok": true}, {"t": "Estoy al parque", "ok": false}, {"t": "Voy el parque", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "What does FEREDE stand for in English?", "opts": [{"t": "A Spanish bank", "ok": false}, {"t": "A Catholic bishops' conference", "ok": false}, {"t": "A federation of evangelical religious entities in Spain", "ok": true}]}, {"q": "How are the figures about 4,400 places of worship and 3,000 municipalities presented in this unit?", "opts": [{"t": "As proven facts with no source", "ok": false}, {"t": "As estimates attributed to FEREDE and cited by Chanak, to be verified", "ok": true}, {"t": "As Catholic Church statistics", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "What does the phrase España vaciada refer to?", "opts": [{"t": "Rural areas that have lost population", "ok": true}, {"t": "A country with no churches", "ok": false}, {"t": "The Spanish Constitution", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "Which attitude does the unit recommend toward Catholic neighbors?", "opts": [{"t": "They should be avoided", "ok": false}, {"t": "They should be corrected publicly", "ok": false}, {"t": "They are friends and not opponents", "ok": true}]}], "verify": ["Según cifras de la FEREDE citadas por Chanak, por verificar: aprox. 4 400 lugares de culto evangélicos en España.", "Según cifras de la FEREDE citadas por Chanak, por verificar: aprox. 3 000 municipios sin testimonio evangélico.", "Founding year of FEREDE (1986) and the 1992 Cooperation Agreement with the Spanish State (Ley 24/1992): confirm before using; the 1978 Constitution's religious freedom clause.", "Meaning and use of 'España vaciada' as a description of depopulated rural areas."], "reading_es": "Mi ciudad tiene una iglesia, una escuela y un parque. La iglesia está cerca del banco. Voy a la biblioteca. En España hay iglesias evangélicas. Pero hay pueblos sin iglesia evangélica. Según la FEREDE, hay muchos pueblos así. Estos datos se deben verificar. Debemos amar a todas las personas. Los católicos y los evangélicos son vecinos. Yo quiero conocer a mis vecinos. Mi ciudad es bonita. Voy a la iglesia el domingo."}, "us:world_language:spanish1:May": {"reading": ["Free time is when people show what they love. In Spanish you can say that you like to jugar al fútbol, nadar, leer, bailar, cantar, ver la tele, escuchar música or pasar tiempo con amigos. When you use gustar with an infinitive, the verb is always singular: Me gusta nadar and Me gusta jugar al béisbol, even if there are several activities, Me gusta nadar y bailar. The verb jugar is irregular because the u changes to ue in most forms: juego, juegas, juega, jugamos, juegan. With a sport, Spanish uses jugar a plus the sport: Juego al fútbol, Jugamos al baloncesto. You can add frequency with siempre, a veces, nunca and los sábados, and you can invite someone with ¿Quieres jugar al fútbol? or ¿Quieres venir a mi casa? The answers are Sí, claro, Me gustaría, or Lo siento, no puedo.", "Popular pastimes in the Spanish-speaking world reveal its variety. Fútbol is popular almost everywhere, baseball is especially strong in the Caribbean and in parts of Central America, and cycling and other sports have great followings in certain countries. Music and dance are also central: families gather for fiestas with guitars, singing and dancing that vary from region to region. These activities happen in the context of community, since many parties are family events and celebrations of local saints or national holidays. Learning to invite and accept invitations is a way of entering that world.", "Free time also helps us understand how churches grew in Latin America in the twentieth century. Evangelical churches began in Latin America in the nineteenth century through missionaries, Bible distributors and educators. During the twentieth century they grew markedly. The Pentecostal movement, which emphasizes the work of the Holy Spirit and lively congregational worship, spread across the region from the early 1900s. It is often mentioned that Pentecostal revival reached Chile around 1909 and Brazil in the 1910s, though exact dates and names of denominations should be verified. In later decades, large evangelical communities appeared in many countries, alongside the historic presence of the Catholic Church.", "Historians point to several reasons for the growth. Local leaders, often from the same neighborhoods as the people they served, preached and led worship without needing foreign missionaries. Lay participation was strong: ordinary members shared their faith, visited neighbors and welcomed newcomers. Congregations met in homes, storefronts and small buildings, often in places where there was little else. Music, singing and prayer in the people's own language and style played a central role. In other words, the growth was linked to the same things you practice in this unit: invitation, participation and shared joy.", "According to estimates cited by Chanak, evangelical presence in Latin America grew from about two percent of the population historically to something between twenty and forty percent today, with large differences from country to country. These figures are estimates, not exact data, and they should be verified; the unit does not give national percentages because they vary widely and depend on the source. What matters for your project is a thoughtful way of seeing them: growth brings joy and also responsibility. Many new believers need teaching, and communities need pastors who know the Bible and care for the poor. Jesus invited, \"Come and see\" (John 1:39). When you ask a friend ¿Quieres venir?, you practice the simplest form of invitation. In the Gap project, think of one way a church could invite a neighbor warmly and respectfully, without pressure and with real friendship."], "glossary": [{"term": "jugar al fútbol", "def": "\"To play soccer\"; jugar is irregular (juego, juegas, juega, jugamos, juegan) and uses a with sports."}, {"term": "Me gusta + infinitive", "def": "\"I like to...\"; the verb gustar stays singular before an infinitive: Me gusta nadar."}, {"term": "siempre / a veces / nunca", "def": "Always / sometimes / never; adverbs of frequency."}, {"term": "¿Quieres...?", "def": "\"Do you want to...?\"; used to invite someone: ¿Quieres jugar?"}, {"term": "pentecostal", "def": "A Christian movement that emphasizes the work of the Holy Spirit and lively worship; it spread across Latin America from the early 1900s."}, {"term": "evangélico", "def": "In Spanish-speaking countries, a Protestant or evangelical Christian, as distinct from Catholic."}, {"term": "participación laica (lay participation)", "def": "Involvement of ordinary church members, not only clergy, in sharing the faith and serving."}, {"term": "discipulado (discipleship)", "def": "Following and learning from Christ over a lifetime."}], "practice": [{"q": "Write four sentences about your free time in Spanish, using gustar with an infinitive, jugar and a frequency word.", "feedback": "A model answer: Me gusta nadar. Juego al fútbol los sábados. A veces canto con mis amigos. Nunca veo la tele por la mañana. Check that gustar stays singular before an infinitive, that jugar is conjugated (juego) and that frequency words are used."}, {"q": "Write a short invitation and two possible replies in Spanish.", "feedback": "A model: ¿Quieres jugar al béisbol el sábado? Sí, claro, me gustaría. / Lo siento, no puedo. The replies show how to accept or refuse politely. Invitations in Hispanic cultures are usually warm and expect a friendly answer."}, {"q": "What factors does the unit mention to explain the growth of evangelical churches in Latin America in the twentieth century?", "feedback": "The unit mentions local leaders, lay participation, worship in the people's own language and style, and congregations meeting in homes and small buildings. It also notes that Pentecostalism spread from the early 1900s, for example in Chile and Brazil, with dates to be verified."}, {"q": "Why are the figures \"from about two percent to twenty to forty percent\" presented with caution, and why does the unit avoid giving figures per country?", "feedback": "They are estimates cited by Chanak, not verified data, and they vary a great deal by country and source. A careful student states the source, says that the numbers are approximate, and avoids claims for individual countries unless a reliable source is available."}], "quiz": [{"q": "Which sentence is correct?", "opts": [{"t": "Me gustan nadar y bailar", "ok": false}, {"t": "Me gusta nadar y bailar", "ok": true}, {"t": "Me gusto nadar y bailar", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "What is the correct form of jugar with \"I\"?", "opts": [{"t": "juego", "ok": true}, {"t": "jugo", "ok": false}, {"t": "jugamos", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "What kind of movement is Pentecostalism in this unit?", "opts": [{"t": "A Catholic religious order", "ok": false}, {"t": "A political party", "ok": false}, {"t": "A movement emphasizing the Holy Spirit and lively worship that spread in the early 1900s", "ok": true}]}, {"q": "How does the unit treat the growth \"from 2 % to 20-40 %\"?", "opts": [{"t": "As exact national statistics", "ok": false}, {"t": "As estimates cited by Chanak that vary by country and need verification", "ok": true}, {"t": "As proof that Catholics are disappearing", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "Which factor helped churches grow, according to the reading?", "opts": [{"t": "Local leaders and lay participation", "ok": true}, {"t": "Only foreign missionaries", "ok": false}, {"t": "Closed doors and no invitations", "ok": false}]}], "verify": ["Estimates cited by Chanak: evangelical presence in Latin America 'from about 2 % historically to 20-40 % today', highly variable by country: confirm source; no national percentages given.", "Pentecostal beginnings in Chile (c. 1909, Methodist Pentecostal Church) and Brazil (1910-1911, Assemblies of God): confirm exact dates and names.", "Influence of Azusa Street (1906) on Latin American Pentecostalism: omitted unless confirmed.", "Statements about popular pastimes by country (baseball in the Caribbean and parts of Central America): confirm generalizations."], "reading_es": "Me gusta jugar al fútbol. Los sábados juego con mis amigos. A veces nado. También me gusta cantar y bailar. ¿Quieres jugar? Hace muchos años, las iglesias evangélicas crecieron en América Latina. Las personas cantaron, oraron y invitaron a sus vecinos. Hoy hay muchas iglesias. Necesitamos aprender la Biblia. Yo invito a mis amigos. Me gusta cantar en la iglesia los domingos. Dios es bueno."}, "us:world_language:spanish1:Jun": {"reading": ["Weather and clothing give you the vocabulary to talk about everyday life and to give simple advice. Spanish uses the verb hacer in many weather expressions: Hace sol, Hace frío, Hace calor, Hace viento, Hace buen tiempo. Other weather verbs stand alone: Llueve (it rains), Nieva (it snows), and Está nublado (it is cloudy). The seasons are la primavera, el verano, el otoño and el invierno. Remember that the seasons are reversed in the Southern Hemisphere: when it is summer in Spain or Mexico, it is winter in Argentina and Chile, so a December holiday in Buenos Aires happens in the heat. To ask about the weather say ¿Qué tiempo hace hoy? and to talk about the weather in a city say En Madrid hace frío en invierno.", "Clothing vocabulary includes la camiseta, la camisa, los pantalones, la falda, el vestido, los zapatos, el abrigo, la chaqueta, el sombrero and el paraguas. Colors are adjectives and must agree: la camiseta roja, las camisetas rojas, el abrigo azul, los pantalones negros. Some colors, such as azul, verde and gris, have one form for both genders. Adjectives usually come after the noun in Spanish: un abrigo azul, not un azul abrigo. With this vocabulary you can give advice or make simple predictions: Hoy hace frío, necesito un abrigo. Mañana llueve, necesito un paraguas. In a forecast you can say Va a nevar and Va a hacer sol, using ir a plus infinitive.", "These ideas lead us to the final theme of the month: the church in Latin America over the long term. Historic Protestant denominations, such as Presbyterians, Methodists, Baptists, Lutherans and Anglicans, began work in Latin America mainly from the nineteenth century on. Missionary groups from different traditions met at the Congress on Christian Work in Latin America, held in Panama in 1916, to coordinate their efforts, a date and title that should be verified. Alongside these historic denominations, Pentecostal and independent churches grew rapidly, and today the evangelical landscape includes many families: historic, Baptist, Reformed, Pentecostal and others, with many congregations that are not part of any large organization.", "Rapid growth also created challenges. In many places there were not enough trained pastors for new congregations. Many new believers had received an initial invitation but not enough teaching to read the Bible carefully. Some communities needed help caring for the poor, orphans and the sick, while others struggled with disagreements among leaders. We should speak of these challenges in general terms and without statistics; every country has its own story, and Catholic and evangelical believers alike face the need to form disciples. Because the Spanish-speaking world is diverse, the best solutions are usually local, led by people who know their neighborhood.", "Discipleship means following Christ over a lifetime, not only making a first decision. Jesus told his followers to \"go and make disciples of all nations, baptizing them... and teaching them to obey everything I have commanded\" (Matthew 28:19-20). Notice that the command includes teaching. Just as you choose clothes according to the weather, a church chooses ways of teaching, caring and serving according to the needs of its community. Weather vocabulary reminds us that conditions change: seasons come and go, and so do the challenges of a community. For your project, write a short forecast in Spanish about the spiritual and practical needs of a place, for example Hoy hace frío en esta comunidad: necesitamos amigos, ayuda y enseñanza, and think about what a faithful church could do next."], "glossary": [{"term": "Hace frío / Hace calor / Hace sol", "def": "It is cold / hot / sunny; weather expressions with hacer."}, {"term": "Llueve / Nieva", "def": "It rains / it snows; weather verbs used on their own."}, {"term": "el abrigo / el paraguas", "def": "The coat / the umbrella; clothing and weather vocabulary."}, {"term": "camiseta roja", "def": "\"Red T-shirt\"; adjectives of color follow the noun and agree in gender and number."}, {"term": "denominación histórica", "def": "A Protestant tradition such as Presbyterian, Methodist, Baptist, Lutheran or Anglican that began work in Latin America mainly from the nineteenth century."}, {"term": "discipulado", "def": "Following Christ and learning from him over a lifetime, including teaching and obedience (Matthew 28:19-20)."}, {"term": "Congreso de Panamá (1916)", "def": "A meeting of Protestant mission groups to coordinate work in Latin America; title and date to be verified."}, {"term": "pastor", "def": "A leader who teaches and cares for a congregation."}], "practice": [{"q": "Write a forecast for tomorrow in three Spanish sentences and say what clothes people need.", "feedback": "A model: Mañana va a llover. Hace frío. Necesito un abrigo y un paraguas. Check that weather expressions are correct, that clothing nouns have the right articles and that you used ir a + infinitive for the future."}, {"q": "Describe in Spanish a person wearing three colored clothing items. Make sure the adjectives agree.", "feedback": "A model: Ella lleva una camiseta roja, una falda azul y zapatos negros. The adjectives must agree in gender and number and follow the noun. Colors such as azul and verde do not change for masculine and feminine, only for plural."}, {"q": "List four families of evangelical churches in Latin America, and explain why the unit avoids giving dates for each denomination by country.", "feedback": "The families are historic denominations, Baptist, Reformed and Pentecostal (among others). The unit says only that historic denominations arrived mainly from the nineteenth century on because exact dates vary by country and must be verified. Overstating details would risk spreading mistakes."}, {"q": "What does discipleship mean in Matthew 28:19-20, and what challenges can growing churches face in providing it?", "feedback": "Discipleship means following Christ over a lifetime and includes baptizing and teaching people to obey his commands. Fast-growing churches may lack trained pastors, Bible teaching and ways of caring for the poor. Challenges should be stated in general terms, without statistics."}], "quiz": [{"q": "Which sentence means \"It is cold and I need a coat\"?", "opts": [{"t": "Tiene frío y necesito un abrigo", "ok": false}, {"t": "Hay frío y necesito un abrigo", "ok": false}, {"t": "Hace frío y necesito un abrigo", "ok": true}]}, {"q": "Which phrase is correct?", "opts": [{"t": "las rojas camisetas", "ok": false}, {"t": "las camisetas rojas", "ok": true}, {"t": "la camisetas rojo", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "In which month would it be winter in Argentina?", "opts": [{"t": "July", "ok": true}, {"t": "January", "ok": false}, {"t": "December", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "According to the unit, when did historic Protestant denominations mainly begin work in Latin America?", "opts": [{"t": "In the fifteenth century", "ok": false}, {"t": "Only after 2000", "ok": false}, {"t": "From the nineteenth century on", "ok": true}]}, {"q": "What does discipleship include according to Matthew 28:19-20?", "opts": [{"t": "Only making a first decision", "ok": false}, {"t": "Teaching people to obey Christ's commands", "ok": true}, {"t": "Avoiding all churches", "ok": false}]}], "verify": ["Panama Congress of 1916 (Congress on Christian Work in Latin America): confirm title, date and organizers.", "Dates of arrival of each denomination by country: stated only as 'from the nineteenth century on'.", "Description of discipleship challenges: general only, no statistics.", "Evangelical families in Latin America (historic, Baptist, Reformed, Pentecostal): presented as general families, with no figures."], "reading_es": "Hoy hace frío. Necesito un abrigo azul. Mañana hace sol. En verano hace calor. Las iglesias en América Latina crecieron mucho. Pero muchas personas necesitan aprender más de la Biblia. Necesitan pastores y amigos. Un discípulo sigue a Jesús toda la vida. Hoy llueve y necesito un paraguas. Mi camiseta es roja. Dios nos ayuda. En verano me gusta nadar. En invierno uso una chaqueta. Yo quiero aprender más."}, "us:world_language:spanish1:Jul": {"reading": ["This final unit gathers everything you have learned in Spanish 1 and prepares you to serve. In the vocabulary of church and community you now know la iglesia, el pastor, la oración, la Biblia, la comunidad, el vecino and la familia. You also know phrases of hospitality: Bienvenido or Bienvenida, Gracias, De nada, Por favor, and the blessing Que Dios te bendiga. To offer help you can say ¿Cómo puedo ayudarte? or ¿Necesitas ayuda?, and with usted, ¿Cómo puedo ayudarle? When you meet someone, you remember what you learned in October: greet according to the time of day, ask ¿Cómo te llamas?, and decide between tú and usted. Your year of Spanish also covered regular verbs, ser, estar, ir, tener, family, school, food, directions, free time and weather.", "Looking back also means looking at the history of the Spanish Bible. In the sixteenth century Casiodoro de Reina, an exile from the Seville area, produced a translation of Scripture printed in Basel in 1569, the Biblia del Oso. Cipriano de Valera, another exile, revised it and his Bible was published in Amsterdam in 1602. In the nineteenth century, Bible societies founded in 1804 and 1816, traveling colporteurs and educators such as Diego Thomson carried Scripture and schools through Spain and Spanish America. In the twentieth century evangelical and Pentecostal churches grew widely in Latin America, bringing new needs for teaching and discipleship.", "Now we look at the present with honest numbers. In Spain, evangelical Christians are a small minority. According to the FEREDE figures cited by Chanak, which are still to be verified, there are about 4,400 evangelical places of worship, and about 3,000 municipalities without an evangelical witness. In Latin America, estimates cited by Chanak say that the evangelical share of the population rose from about two percent historically to between twenty and forty percent today, with differences from country to country. Together they describe a gap, la brecha: in some places a lack of presence, in others a lack of teaching and discipleship.", "The project \"La Brecha y Cómo Alcanzar,\" or \"The Gap and How to Reach It,\" asks you to apply what you have learned in a practical and respectful way. You will identify a gap in your own community, perhaps a Spanish-speaking family, a neighborhood or a town where few people know the Bible, and propose a realistic way to reach it. The Chanak rubric weighs your work in three parts: thirty percent for knowledge, which includes accurate history and vocabulary; forty percent for execution, which means the quality and usefulness of what you produce; and thirty percent for local connection, which means whether your plan fits real people and a real place.", "Finally, remember the attitude that should guide any plan. Respect Catholic and all other neighbors as people made in the image of God (Genesis 1:27). Work with local churches and leaders; do not act alone. Do not name or identify real minors or private persons in your examples. Start with small steps of friendship: a greeting, a meal, an offer of help, a Bible in the language of the home. Remember Psalm 119:105: God's word is a lamp to guide our steps. If Reina and Valera worked for decades so that Spanish speakers could read Scripture, a student can begin this year with a simple, kind sentence: Hola, me llamo... ¿Cómo puedo ayudarte? That sentence, spoken with humility, may be the first step toward closing the gap."], "glossary": [{"term": "la brecha (the gap)", "def": "In the project, the distance between a community's needs (presence, teaching, Bibles) and what is available."}, {"term": "Bienvenido/a", "def": "\"Welcome\"; adjust the ending to the person's gender."}, {"term": "Que Dios te bendiga", "def": "\"May God bless you\"; a common blessing in Spanish."}, {"term": "¿Cómo puedo ayudarte?", "def": "\"How can I help you?\"; use ayudarle with usted."}, {"term": "colportor", "def": "A traveling distributor of Bibles and Christian books in the nineteenth century."}, {"term": "rúbrica Chanak (30/40/30)", "def": "The project rubric: 30 % knowledge, 40 % execution and 30 % local connection."}, {"term": "FEREDE", "def": "Federación de Entidades Religiosas Evangélicas de España; its figures, as cited by Chanak, are to be verified."}, {"term": "discipulado", "def": "Following Christ and teaching others to follow him over a lifetime."}], "practice": [{"q": "Write a 6-line dialogue in Spanish in which you welcome a new neighbor, ask how you can help, and offer a blessing.", "feedback": "A model: Buenas tardes. Me llamo Ana. Bienvenido al barrio. ¿Cómo puedo ayudarte? / Necesito una tienda cerca. / La tienda está a la derecha. / Que Dios te bendiga. The dialogue should use correct greetings, a polite offer and a respectful close, with tú or usted chosen carefully."}, {"q": "Draw a timeline in words (five dates or periods) from Reina's Bible to the present, and say which dates the team still has to verify.", "feedback": "A good timeline lists the Biblia del Oso (1569), Valera's Bible (1602), the Bible societies (1804 and 1816), Thomson and colporteurs in the nineteenth century, and the growth of evangelical churches in the twentieth century. It should flag dates that need confirmation, such as Pentecostal beginnings and Panama 1916."}, {"q": "Identify the gap in your community in four sentences, without naming real people or minors, and propose one respectful first step.", "feedback": "A good answer describes a group or place in general terms, for example a neighborhood with Spanish-speaking families who may not have a Bible at home, and proposes a respectful first step such as a bilingual welcome event or a visit with a local church. It does not identify individuals and avoids pressure."}, {"q": "Explain the Chanak project rubric (30/40/30) and what each part rewards.", "feedback": "Knowledge (30 %) rewards accurate history, vocabulary and use of sources. Execution (40 %) rewards the quality, clarity and usefulness of what you produce. Local connection (30 %) rewards a plan that fits real people and places and works with local churches. The largest part is execution because a plan must be carried out."}], "quiz": [{"q": "Which sentence is a polite offer of help to a friend?", "opts": [{"t": "¿Cómo puedo ayudarte?", "ok": true}, {"t": "¿Cómo te llamas ayuda?", "ok": false}, {"t": "Yo ayudas a ti", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "What are the three parts of the Chanak project rubric?", "opts": [{"t": "Speed 50 %, length 25 %, color 25 %", "ok": false}, {"t": "Grammar 100 %", "ok": false}, {"t": "Knowledge 30 %, execution 40 %, local connection 30 %", "ok": true}]}, {"q": "According to the figures cited by Chanak and attributed to the FEREDE, how are the numbers of places of worship and municipalities to be used?", "opts": [{"t": "As verified government statistics", "ok": false}, {"t": "As estimates to be verified, with the source named", "ok": true}, {"t": "As reasons to criticize Catholic neighbors", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "Which sequence of events is in the correct order?", "opts": [{"t": "Biblia del Oso (1569), Valera's revision (1602), Bible societies in the 1800s", "ok": true}, {"t": "Bible societies, Biblia del Oso, Valera's revision", "ok": false}, {"t": "Valera's revision, Biblia del Oso, Thomson", "ok": false}]}, {"q": "Which attitude should guide the project according to the unit?", "opts": [{"t": "Competition with other churches", "ok": false}, {"t": "Acting alone and naming private individuals", "ok": false}, {"t": "Respect, humility and cooperation with local churches", "ok": true}]}], "verify": ["All earlier unit verifications apply; the timeline must use only confirmed dates (1569, 1602, 1804, 1816).", "FEREDE figures (aprox. 4 400 lugares de culto; aprox. 3 000 municipios sin testimonio evangélico): según cifras de la FEREDE citadas por Chanak, por verificar.", "Estimate cited by Chanak for Latin America (from about 2 % to 20-40 %), variable by country: confirm source.", "Final project examples must not name or identify real students or minors."], "reading_es": "Hola. Soy un estudiante de español. Digo: Bienvenido. Gracias. De nada. Que Dios te bendiga. ¿Cómo puedo ayudarte? Reina y Valera hicieron la Biblia en español. Hoy hay lugares sin iglesia evangélica. Es una brecha. Yo quiero ayudar con respeto. Hablo con amigos. Oro por mi comunidad. Mi iglesia es una familia. Quiero conocer a mis vecinos. Gracias, Señor, por tu Palabra."}});
